# Acoustic fire suppression
Experiments on acoustic fire suppression (using sound to extinguish fires) have been carried out by NASA, but it remains strictly experimental. In acoustic fire suppression, low-frequency sound waves (around 20-80 Hz) are played with constant volume. The pressure waves vibrate and push oxygen molecules away from the fuel source to interrupt combustion. Obviously there is also no waste product or residual chemical from Acoustic fire suppression too. In 0g, low velocity winds draw oxygen into the flames, feeding and growing it. If the acoustic amplitude is too low to fully extinguish the fire, the induced air movement could intensify the burn instead of putting it out. Additionally, as the sound waves displace gas without cooling the fuel source, cool flames may reform from residual heat. 

